1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,027
Three nightly jobs on a clock.

2
00:00:02,347 --> 00:00:04,608
Prepare the data at 1 in the morning.

3
00:00:04,928 --> 00:00:06,037
Train at 2.

4
00:00:06,357 --> 00:00:07,573
Deploy at 3.

5
00:00:07,893 --> 00:00:09,216
For months, it works.

6
00:00:11,717 --> 00:00:14,896
Then one night,
preparation takes 75 minutes.

7
00:00:15,216 --> 00:00:19,290
At 2, training starts anyway,
on a file that is only half written.

8
00:00:19,610 --> 00:00:22,639
At 3, deployment ships
whatever training produced.

9
00:00:23,833 --> 00:00:26,137
Every job exits with status zero.

10
00:00:26,457 --> 00:00:27,566
Nothing failed.

11
00:00:27,886 --> 00:00:31,470
And the endpoint now serves a model trained
on half the data.

12
00:00:31,790 --> 00:00:33,241
Nothing was right either.

13
00:00:33,483 --> 00:00:35,899
The lecture opens on the same problem:

14
00:00:35,899 --> 00:00:40,032
four steps of the cheese app,
each run by a person at a terminal.

15
00:00:40,352 --> 00:00:42,187
How do we execute these steps?

16
00:00:42,507 --> 00:00:44,150
Can we automate this flow?

17
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:47,124
To automate it,
you need a different instruction.

18
00:00:47,444 --> 00:00:48,831
Not: train at 2.

19
00:00:49,151 --> 00:00:51,839
Instead:
train when preparation has finished.

20
00:00:52,159 --> 00:00:54,015
That is what a pipeline is for.

21
00:00:54,250 --> 00:00:55,366
Here is the plan:

22
00:00:55,366 --> 00:00:59,434
graphs, wires, overlap, order, deployment,
and the cheese app.

23
00:00:59,683 --> 00:01:03,758
The gray tag, top right, names
the slides behind each idea.

24
00:01:04,078 --> 00:01:05,763
Nothing here is a live run.

25
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,637
If an option schedules steps at fixed times,
think race:

26
00:01:09,637 --> 00:01:11,781
a clock knows nothing about data.

27
00:01:13,117 --> 00:01:17,341
Workflow management, in the lecture's words,
knows which step needs which.

28
00:01:17,661 --> 00:01:22,226
The structure that records this
is a directed acyclic graph, or DAG.

29
00:01:22,467 --> 00:01:26,670
Directed, because data flows one way,
from producer to consumer.

30
00:01:26,990 --> 00:01:30,062
Acyclic,
because no step ever waits on itself.

31
00:01:30,382 --> 00:01:33,987
Tasks are the nodes;
data dependencies are the edges.

32
00:01:34,234 --> 00:01:37,391
Vertex AI Pipelines runs
these graphs for you.

33
00:01:37,711 --> 00:01:40,957
You describe the graph
with Kubeflow Pipelines,

34
00:01:40,957 --> 00:01:44,687
the kfp library,
and there is no Kubernetes to manage.

35
00:01:45,007 --> 00:01:47,780
Today's console calls it Agent Platform.

36
00:01:48,017 --> 00:01:51,814
Here is the toy pipeline from the slides
and the demo repo.

37
00:01:52,134 --> 00:01:55,684
Three Python functions
marked @dsl.component:

38
00:01:55,684 --> 00:01:57,894
square, add and square root.

39
00:01:58,214 --> 00:02:02,225
A fourth,
marked @dsl.pipeline, wires them together.

40
00:02:02,467 --> 00:02:06,414
When the pipeline function runs,
the component calls compute nothing.

41
00:02:06,734 --> 00:02:10,955
Calling square on a,
the pipeline's input, which defaults to 3,

42
00:02:10,955 --> 00:02:15,310
creates a task: a node in the graph,
with an empty output socket.

43
00:02:15,630 --> 00:02:16,782
No number yet.

44
00:02:17,017 --> 00:02:19,812
Line by line, four calls drop four nodes.

45
00:02:20,132 --> 00:02:23,949
Square is called twice,
so one component gives two tasks;

46
00:02:23,949 --> 00:02:25,892
the second is named square-2.

47
00:02:26,212 --> 00:02:29,241
The function ran once,
and produced only a graph.

48
00:02:29,484 --> 00:02:32,407
The compiler presses
that graph into a YAML file.

49
00:02:32,727 --> 00:02:36,490
The Vertex AI SDK,
the lecture's remote control,

50
00:02:36,490 --> 00:02:38,764
submits it as a pipeline job.

51
00:02:39,084 --> 00:02:41,175
Your laptop compiles and submits.

52
00:02:41,495 --> 00:02:42,924
Vertex executes.

53
00:02:44,284 --> 00:02:46,291
Only now, on Google's machines,

54
00:02:46,291 --> 00:02:49,788
does each task get a container,
and do numbers appear.

55
00:02:52,067 --> 00:02:56,789
If an option says calling train
in the pipeline function trains the model,

56
00:02:56,789 --> 00:02:59,086
think again: it only creates a task.

57
00:02:59,406 --> 00:03:02,286
Nothing runs until the compiled job runs.

58
00:03:03,433 --> 00:03:04,756
Look at the add call.

59
00:03:05,076 --> 00:03:08,532
For x, it passes
the first square task, .output.

60
00:03:08,852 --> 00:03:11,454
For y, the second task, .output.

61
00:03:11,774 --> 00:03:13,225
So what is being passed?

62
00:03:13,466 --> 00:03:14,490
Not a number.

63
00:03:14,810 --> 00:03:18,204
.output is a placeholder
for a value that exists

64
00:03:18,204 --> 00:03:20,890
only after the upstream task succeeds.

65
00:03:21,210 --> 00:03:22,938
Plugging it in draws a wire.

66
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:26,561
The compiled YAML
writes down each wire twice:

67
00:03:26,561 --> 00:03:29,722
add's dependentTasks
list square and square-2,

68
00:03:29,722 --> 00:03:32,402
and each input names its producer task.

69
00:03:34,966 --> 00:03:40,278
At run time, square turns green,
and only then does 9 ride the wire into add.

70
00:03:40,598 --> 00:03:44,481
One argument gave add both its data
and its dependency.

71
00:03:44,716 --> 00:03:47,495
With several outputs,
you name the one you want:

72
00:03:47,495 --> 00:03:50,562
.outputs["name"],
with the output's name in brackets.

73
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:52,080
Now the contrast.

74
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:56,155
Often the output is itself a path:
the file this run wrote.

75
00:03:56,475 --> 00:04:00,528
With the wire, train gets that exact path,
after prep succeeds.

76
00:04:00,848 --> 00:04:03,960
Hard-code a path instead,
and there is no wire:

77
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:06,608
train starts right away, alongside prep.

78
00:04:06,850 --> 00:04:10,925
The file there is last week's,
and the step succeeds on stale data.

79
00:04:11,245 --> 00:04:15,148
A guessed path looks like a dependency
to you, not to the pipeline.

80
00:04:15,383 --> 00:04:18,800
For order without data,
.after() adds a dashed edge:

81
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:23,596
the task waits for the other one to succeed,
but nothing travels down it.

82
00:04:23,833 --> 00:04:24,833
Try one.

83
00:04:25,113 --> 00:04:26,478
Pause here if you want.

84
00:04:32,433 --> 00:04:33,433
B.

85
00:04:33,585 --> 00:04:36,305
Naming the output hands
over this run's file,

86
00:04:36,305 --> 00:04:37,574
and creates the wait.

87
00:04:37,894 --> 00:04:39,275
A hard-codes a path:

88
00:04:39,275 --> 00:04:43,484
no edge, so train may start
at once and read last run's file.

89
00:04:43,733 --> 00:04:47,893
C waits, but it reads a path you assumed,
not the one split reported.

90
00:04:48,213 --> 00:04:54,400
D fails before anything runs:
with two outputs, kfp refuses plain .output.

91
00:04:54,650 --> 00:04:57,087
If the question says .output, think:

92
00:04:57,087 --> 00:05:00,133
waits for success,
and gets this run's value.

93
00:05:00,453 --> 00:05:03,823
A hard-coded or guessed path:
no edge, maybe stale.

94
00:05:04,143 --> 00:05:06,447
.after(): order, but no data.

95
00:05:07,583 --> 00:05:08,948
Back to the toy graph.

96
00:05:09,268 --> 00:05:12,330
No path of wires connects
the two square tasks,

97
00:05:12,330 --> 00:05:15,391
so Vertex is free
to run them at the same time.

98
00:05:15,633 --> 00:05:16,806
Add is a join.

99
00:05:17,126 --> 00:05:20,007
Its YAML lists both squares
as dependentTasks,

100
00:05:20,007 --> 00:05:22,950
so it starts
only when the second one finishes.

101
00:05:23,616 --> 00:05:27,109
The same shape, one size up:
one preprocessing task,

102
00:05:27,109 --> 00:05:32,214
two trainings that read its output,
and a comparison that reads both models.

103
00:05:32,450 --> 00:05:36,226
A task lights up
when every incoming wire carries a token.

104
00:05:36,546 --> 00:05:38,322
Both trainings start together,

105
00:05:38,322 --> 00:05:41,815
and the comparison starts exactly
when the slower one ends.

106
00:05:42,433 --> 00:05:46,465
Add an edge the data does not need:
same answer, longer run.

107
00:05:47,450 --> 00:05:49,939
Replace those wires with guessed paths,

108
00:05:49,939 --> 00:05:54,469
and the comparison starts at time zero
and reads half-finished results.

109
00:05:54,789 --> 00:05:55,919
That is a race.

110
00:05:56,166 --> 00:06:00,528
Maximum safe overlap means
exactly the edges the data needs,

111
00:06:00,528 --> 00:06:02,054
no more and no fewer.

112
00:06:02,374 --> 00:06:04,486
The run then lasts its longest path.

113
00:06:04,806 --> 00:06:07,900
If an upstream task fails,
the join never runs.

114
00:06:08,150 --> 00:06:09,729
Your turn to compute one.

115
00:06:10,049 --> 00:06:11,414
Pause here if you want.

116
00:06:17,350 --> 00:06:18,843
B, 16 minutes.

117
00:06:19,163 --> 00:06:23,323
The longest path is ingest, clean,
the big training, and compare.

118
00:06:23,643 --> 00:06:26,118
2, plus 3, plus 10, plus 1.

119
00:06:26,438 --> 00:06:29,830
The small training
and the report overlap with the big one.

120
00:06:30,066 --> 00:06:32,157
A adds every task in sequence.

121
00:06:32,477 --> 00:06:35,186
D runs the two trainings one
after the other.

122
00:06:35,506 --> 00:06:36,871
C ignores the edges:

123
00:06:36,871 --> 00:06:41,032
training needs clean data,
and compare waits for both models.

124
00:06:41,266 --> 00:06:45,063
If there is no path of wires
between two steps, think parallel.

125
00:06:45,383 --> 00:06:49,202
If a step consumes several outputs,
it waits for all of them.

126
00:06:50,350 --> 00:06:53,379
Now the sequence: preprocess, train, deploy.

127
00:06:53,699 --> 00:06:57,006
Here are four ways to make each step wait,
on one clock.

128
00:06:57,250 --> 00:06:59,021
Lane one, cron offsets.

129
00:06:59,341 --> 00:07:01,602
Lane two, background & jobs.

130
00:07:01,922 --> 00:07:06,449
Lane three, an event starts training
when preprocessing writes its output,

131
00:07:06,449 --> 00:07:09,325
and deploy takes the best model
in the tracker.

132
00:07:09,645 --> 00:07:11,650
Lane four, pipeline edges.

133
00:07:11,900 --> 00:07:13,884
Roll the length of preprocessing.

134
00:07:14,204 --> 00:07:15,249
40 minutes.

135
00:07:15,569 --> 00:07:16,849
Then 75.

136
00:07:17,169 --> 00:07:18,407
Then 55.

137
00:07:18,650 --> 00:07:22,062
The clock lane is fine at 40,
and broken at 75:

138
00:07:22,062 --> 00:07:24,602
training reads a half-written file.

139
00:07:24,922 --> 00:07:28,741
The background lane is broken on every roll,
because nothing waits.

140
00:07:28,983 --> 00:07:30,818
The tracker lane fails twice.

141
00:07:31,138 --> 00:07:35,212
The event can fire on the first file,
before preprocessing is done.

142
00:07:35,532 --> 00:07:39,714
And the tracker's best model
is the best of all runs, not this run's.

143
00:07:40,034 --> 00:07:42,636
Only the edge lane is right on every roll.

144
00:07:42,956 --> 00:07:44,215
It just stretches.

145
00:07:45,250 --> 00:07:50,131
The cheese app pipeline does exactly this:
processing after collection,

146
00:07:50,131 --> 00:07:51,943
training after processing,

147
00:07:51,943 --> 00:07:55,149
deployment after training,
each with .after().

148
00:07:55,383 --> 00:07:58,455
In the compiled YAML they
become dependentTasks.

149
00:07:58,775 --> 00:08:00,751
And the README says it plainly:

150
00:08:00,751 --> 00:08:04,194
each step starts
only after the previous one succeeds.

151
00:08:04,433 --> 00:08:06,097
One more, a subtle one.

152
00:08:06,417 --> 00:08:09,169
Some steps only launch work
on another machine.

153
00:08:09,489 --> 00:08:12,113
Remember exactly what an edge waits for.

154
00:08:12,433 --> 00:08:13,799
Pause here if you want.

155
00:08:21,633 --> 00:08:22,633
B.

156
00:08:22,785 --> 00:08:27,201
An edge waits for the task,
and this task succeeded after 5 seconds.

157
00:08:27,521 --> 00:08:30,366
The pipeline never sees the job it launched,

158
00:08:30,366 --> 00:08:33,857
so A is wrong,
and deploy may ship the previous model.

159
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:36,233
C: .after() needs no data.

160
00:08:36,553 --> 00:08:41,311
D: .after() is enforced,
written into the YAML as a dependent task.

161
00:08:41,550 --> 00:08:44,195
The repo's training step is such a launcher.

162
00:08:44,515 --> 00:08:49,284
With sync=True, it waits for its job,
and fails if the job fails,

163
00:08:49,284 --> 00:08:52,366
so deploy never runs on a failed training.

164
00:08:52,686 --> 00:08:55,289
A recent commit made that wait explicit.

165
00:08:55,533 --> 00:08:59,007
If an option uses cron offsets,
background commands,

166
00:08:59,007 --> 00:09:03,149
or the best model from the tracker,
think race or wrong model.

167
00:09:03,469 --> 00:09:08,120
Edges from preprocess to train
to deploy are correct by construction.

168
00:09:09,267 --> 00:09:15,111
The lecture draws deployment in five steps:
get the saved model, update its signature,

169
00:09:15,111 --> 00:09:19,528
upload it to a bucket, register it,
and deploy it to an endpoint.

170
00:09:19,767 --> 00:09:24,418
The signature step moves
preprocessing into the model:

171
00:09:24,418 --> 00:09:31,138
decode the JPEG, convert to floats,
resize to 224 by 224, then call the model.

172
00:09:31,458 --> 00:09:35,831
The slides save it one way,
the repo with the Keras ExportArchive.

173
00:09:36,067 --> 00:09:38,392
Registering creates a catalog card.

174
00:09:38,712 --> 00:09:42,211
It records where the files are,
and which container serves them.

175
00:09:42,531 --> 00:09:44,942
Nothing is running, and it costs nothing.

176
00:09:45,262 --> 00:09:47,608
Can you call it? Not yet.

177
00:09:47,850 --> 00:09:50,047
Next, the endpoint: an address.

178
00:09:50,367 --> 00:09:54,613
The repo reuses cheese-app-endpoint
if it exists, or creates it.

179
00:09:54,884 --> 00:09:59,044
Deploy slides a machine under that address
and sends it all the traffic.

180
00:09:59,364 --> 00:10:01,666
From now on, you pay for that machine

181
00:10:01,666 --> 00:10:04,654
while it is deployed,
even when nobody calls it.

182
00:10:05,650 --> 00:10:09,253
To predict,
the client wraps each JPEG in base64,

183
00:10:09,253 --> 00:10:11,239
under the key bytes_inputs.

184
00:10:11,559 --> 00:10:15,224
The signature unfolds it,
the model returns four scores,

185
00:10:15,224 --> 00:10:17,384
and the largest names the cheese.

186
00:10:17,634 --> 00:10:19,371
Cleanup undeploys the model,

187
00:10:19,371 --> 00:10:22,349
so the machine stops,
then deletes the endpoint.

188
00:10:22,669 --> 00:10:25,463
The registry card and the bucket files stay.

189
00:10:25,700 --> 00:10:28,943
The pipeline's deploy step
makes the same two calls.

190
00:10:29,263 --> 00:10:31,259
And by the slides' rule of thumb,

191
00:10:31,259 --> 00:10:34,767
Cloud Run fits
when the model is one part of a bigger app.

192
00:10:35,017 --> 00:10:37,484
If the question says registered, think:

193
00:10:37,484 --> 00:10:40,585
not callable until it is deployed
to an endpoint.

194
00:10:40,905 --> 00:10:45,321
If it says deployed, think:
a machine is billing until you undeploy it.

195
00:10:45,641 --> 00:10:47,284
Then delete the endpoint too.

196
00:10:48,433 --> 00:10:53,297
Now the whole cheese app pipeline:
collector, processor, training, deploy.

197
00:10:53,617 --> 00:10:55,857
Each gets a green check as it succeeds.

198
00:10:56,100 --> 00:10:57,423
Two kinds of steps.

199
00:10:57,743 --> 00:11:03,004
The collector and processor are container
components that pull published images

200
00:11:03,004 --> 00:11:07,199
from a registry, Docker Hub in this repo,
the Artifact Registry

201
00:11:07,199 --> 00:11:11,460
on the slides, because Google's machines
cannot see your laptop.

202
00:11:11,780 --> 00:11:16,985
Training and deploy are Python components
that call the Vertex AI SDK.

203
00:11:17,233 --> 00:11:19,793
So why .after(), and not .output?

204
00:11:20,113 --> 00:11:22,012
The steps never call each other.

205
00:11:22,332 --> 00:11:27,665
Each reads from the bucket and writes back:
raw.zip, clean.zip, then the model/ folder.

206
00:11:27,985 --> 00:11:30,908
The pipeline only decides
when each step starts.

207
00:11:31,150 --> 00:11:33,318
So deploy finds the model by name,

208
00:11:33,318 --> 00:11:36,889
in the model/ folder,
and the edges make that name safe.

209
00:11:37,209 --> 00:11:40,467
Delete one,
and deploy may grab last run's model:

210
00:11:40,467 --> 00:11:42,328
the stale read from earlier.

211
00:11:42,566 --> 00:11:46,289
One difference from the slides:
on the main branch today,

212
00:11:46,289 --> 00:11:51,057
training downloads its own dataset,
so these edges carry order, not data.

213
00:11:51,300 --> 00:11:55,396
On AWS,
SageMaker Pipelines runs the same idea.

214
00:11:55,716 --> 00:11:59,986
The training step's input points
at the processing step's output property,

215
00:11:59,986 --> 00:12:01,774
and that reference is the edge.

216
00:12:02,266 --> 00:12:06,160
Shuffle the pipeline's list of steps,
and the graph does not move:

217
00:12:06,160 --> 00:12:08,048
order comes from the references.

218
00:12:08,283 --> 00:12:12,294
If the question says SageMaker Pipelines,
think: the same DAG.

219
00:12:12,614 --> 00:12:15,131
Referencing a step's properties
makes the edge.

220
00:12:16,267 --> 00:12:17,398
Seven rules.

221
00:12:17,718 --> 00:12:19,446
One: pipelines are DAGs.

222
00:12:19,766 --> 00:12:22,390
Calling a component creates a task, a node.

223
00:12:22,710 --> 00:12:25,590
Nothing runs until the compiled job runs.

224
00:12:25,834 --> 00:12:31,295
Two: .output, or a named output,
makes the edge and passes this run's value.

225
00:12:31,615 --> 00:12:34,858
Three: a guessed path makes no edge,
and may be stale.

226
00:12:35,100 --> 00:12:37,617
Four: .after() orders without data.

227
00:12:37,937 --> 00:12:40,987
Five: no path between tasks means parallel,

228
00:12:40,987 --> 00:12:45,810
a join waits for all its inputs,
and the run lasts its longest path.

229
00:12:46,050 --> 00:12:48,590
Six: order comes from edges, not clocks,

230
00:12:48,590 --> 00:12:51,576
and a step
that launches work must wait for it.

231
00:12:51,817 --> 00:12:56,980
Seven: upload to the Model Registry,
create an endpoint, deploy, then predict.

232
00:12:57,300 --> 00:12:58,857
Billing starts at deploy.

233
00:12:59,177 --> 00:13:02,569
SageMaker builds the same DAG
from step properties.

234
00:13:02,817 --> 00:13:07,694
And the lecture's chain:
a recipe in the DSL, printed as YAML,

235
00:13:07,694 --> 00:13:11,548
submitted by the SDK,
run on the Kubeflow engine,

236
00:13:11,548 --> 00:13:16,662
and managed by Vertex AI Pipelines,
with no Kubernetes to set up.

237
00:13:16,900 --> 00:13:19,887
When you are done,
undeploy and delete the endpoint.

238
00:13:20,207 --> 00:13:21,551
Good luck on the quiz.

